Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "religion-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Religion in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Religion by political region",infoboxKicker:"Religious traditions and legal orders of Japanese Asia",
    lead:"Religion in the Co-Prosperity Sphere encompasses State Shinto and Japanese Buddhism; Korean Christianity and Buddhism; Chinese Confucian, Buddhist, Daoist, popular, and Christian traditions; Mongolian and Tibetan Buddhism; Filipino Catholicism; Indonesian, Malayan, and Uyghur Islam; Thai and Burmese Buddhism; Russian Orthodoxy; and numerous local rites. The Sphere has no common creed, religious sovereign, or ecclesiastical law. Japan's leadership is political and strategic rather than sacramental.",
    canon:"The emperor as Emperor of Japan rather than universal ruler, non-imposition of State Shinto, national religious traditions, protected Japanese worship abroad, Thai protection of Malay Islam, Manchukuoan pluralism, Vietnamese religious layering, Mongolian Buddhism, and associated-state sovereignty are established. A common legal doctrine is synthesis; detailed national statutes, censuses, concordats, and jurisdictional cases remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"diversity",title:"Religious geography",html:"<p>The Sphere joins societies whose sacred traditions are older than modern Japanese rule and whose institutions help define national legitimacy. No single clergy, liturgy, scripture, or sacred language could plausibly represent them all.</p>"},
      {id:"headship",title:"Political headship without a common cult",html:"<p>The Japanese emperor is sovereign of Japan and ceremonial patron of concord, not emperor or high priest of the associated states. Respect for Japan's treaty position does not constitute religious submission.</p>"},
      {id:"national",title:"National jurisdiction",html:"<p>Member governments regulate recognition, property, schools, marriage, charities, clergy, holidays, and public ritual under their own constitutional orders. Japan intervenes most readily when conflict threatens bases, treaty residents, foreign policy, or system security.</p>"},
      {id:"catholic-communion",title:"Catholic communion across national settlements",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-k2r7m writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:29:06-06:00 --><p>The Sphere's rejection of a common creed left Catholic communities under different civil settlements without dividing them into national churches. A Filipino, Vietnamese, Korean, Chinese, Japanese, or Micronesian local church remained in communion with Rome and under the bishop or other ordinary competent in Catholic law. The size and public weight of Philippine Catholicism did not give its hierarchy jurisdiction over Catholics elsewhere in the Sphere, and political association with Japan did not make the Holy See a foreign government directing their national allegiance.</p><p>National constitutions, treaties, and administrative law could recognize legal personality, property, schools, hospitals, charities, public worship, marriage effects, visas, and foreign remittances. Episcopal appointment, diocesan jurisdiction, doctrine, sacraments, clerical faculties, and the internal government of religious institutes arose from ecclesiastical authority. A treaty guarantee was therefore not a concordat unless the Holy See was actually a party, and a concordat regulated only the matters committed by its text. Neither instrument by itself proved unrestricted pastoral freedom in local administration.</p><p>Comparison requires the document that governed the disputed act: treaty or constitution, canonical provision, episcopal statute, pastoral letter, diplomatic note, school rule, property record, subsidy condition, police order, or judicial appeal. Parishioners, catechists, women religious, lay teachers, nurses, minority Christians, and people outside the Church can show how formal guarantees operated beyond chancery files. Institutional continuity might reflect pastoral attachment, legal immunity, state patronage, accommodation, or several causes together.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Sacred institutions",html:"<p>Temples, shrines, churches, mosques, monasteries, schools, charities, courts, cemeteries, pilgrimage sites, and religious estates receive national or treaty protection with unequal autonomy.</p>"},
      {id:"family",title:"Family and personal law",html:"<p>Marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption, burial, and education can fall under civil, customary, Islamic, Buddhist, Catholic, Orthodox, or mixed systems. Cross-national households create recognition problems without creating one Sphere family code.</p>"},
      {id:"forces",title:"Armed forces and chaplaincy",html:"<p>National forces preserve their own rites and pastoral arrangements. Japanese units abroad remain Japanese institutions, while local auxiliaries and associated armies require clergy suited to their populations.</p>"},
      {id:"foreign",title:"Foreign religious ties",html:"<p>Rome, Orthodox hierarchies, international Buddhist networks, Islamic scholarship, missions, and overseas diaspora institutions connect believers beyond the Sphere. Governments distinguish worship and education from foreign political direction.</p>"},
      {id:"legitimacy",title:"Religious pluralism and legitimacy",html:"<p>Japan presents non-assimilation as proof that its order protects Asian civilizations rather than erasing them. Critics note that protection remains conditional upon political loyalty and does not prevent coercion inside integral territories.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"religious-nonassimilation-geacps.html",kicker:"Governing doctrine",label:"Religious Non-assimilation Doctrine"},{href:"sacred-institutions-geacps.html",kicker:"Legal protection",label:"Sacred Institutions in the Sphere"},{href:"religious-family-law-geacps.html",kicker:"Personal status",label:"Religious Schools and Family Law"},{href:"military-chaplaincy-geacps.html",kicker:"Armed forces",label:"Military Chaplaincy"},{href:"state-shinto-outside-japan.html",kicker:"Japanese worship abroad",label:"State Shinto outside Japan"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Political order",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Common Sphere religion","None"],["Religious sovereign","None; emperor is sovereign of Japan"],["Principal legal authority","National governments"],["State Shinto outside Japan","Protected Japanese institution, not universal cult"],["Sacred institutions","National and treaty protection"],["Family law","National, civil, customary, or confessional"],["Foreign religious ties","Permitted under political supervision"],["Complete statutes and religious census","Open"]]
  }),

  "religious-nonassimilation-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Religious Non-assimilation Doctrine in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Religious policy",infoboxKicker:"Separation of Japanese leadership from religious conversion",
    lead:"The religious non-assimilation doctrine is the mature principle that membership in the Co-Prosperity Sphere and acceptance of Japanese political leadership do not require adoption of State Shinto, Japanese Buddhism, imperial household rites, or any other Japanese creed. Associated states remain responsible for their own religious settlements, while Japan claims authority to defend the common order rather than define the sacred truth of every member people.",
    canon:"The emperor's exclusively Japanese sovereignty, non-imposition of State Shinto, continued Filipino Catholic, Mongolian and Tibetan Buddhist, Uyghur Muslim, Russian Orthodox, Thai and Burmese Buddhist, and Chinese traditions are established. The doctrine's name and systematic legal formulation are synthesis; promulgation, Japanese title, treaty clauses, and judicial precedents remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"basis",title:"Constitutional basis",html:"<p>The Sphere is an association of states, not an enlargement of Japanese territory. Its peoples owe treaty duties to a Japanese-led order without becoming subjects of the Japanese emperor.</p>"},
      {id:"negative",title:"What Japan does not require",html:"<p>Associated rulers need not enter State Shinto, worship at Japanese shrines, accept a Japanese religious calendar, dissolve foreign hierarchies, or replace national personal law with Japanese rites.</p>"},
      {id:"positive",title:"What Japan does require",html:"<p>Governments must protect Japanese treaty residents, prevent sacred institutions from becoming bases of hostile military action, honor collective security, and maintain public order.</p>"},
      {id:"courtesy",title:"Courtesy without confession",html:"<p>Officials may attend each other's ceremonies as diplomatic courtesy. A Manchukuoan minister at a Shinto observance or Japanese envoy at a Buddhist rite does not thereby profess the other's faith.</p>"},
      {id:"immunity",title:"Religious immunity and Catholic jurisdiction",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-b5n9q writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:20:35-06:00 --><p>Nonassimilation protected more than the absence of a formal conversion law. A state could still coerce religious profession through mandatory shrine acts, oath formulas, school observances, employment conditions, household registration, or denial of civil benefits. Diplomatic attendance was compatible with religious immunity only where the act, surrounding instruction, and freedom to abstain made clear that no profession of faith was required. The label <em>courtesy</em> could not settle every ceremony in advance.</p><p>For Catholics, the doctrine preserved public space for Filipino and smaller communities while leaving their ecclesiastical government under Catholic law. National government could regulate civil personality, property, education, public order, visas, and foreign exchange. It could not create episcopal jurisdiction, determine doctrine, appoint a bishop by its own authority, or make the Philippine hierarchy a bloc-wide national church. Communion with Rome did not convert Catholic citizens into Vatican political dependants.</p><p>Implementation is shown by oath and ceremony texts, school rules, service orders, shrine-contribution records, exemption procedures, personnel files, sanctions, appeals, and testimony from families, pupils, workers, clergy, and religious communities. Associated-state protection and the weaker position of integral-territory cultures require separate study. An administration could avoid the language of conversion while still making refusal costly enough to defeat practical religious freedom.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"models",title:"National applications",html:"<p>Thailand protects Malay Islam beneath royal paramountcy. Manchukuo preserves court rites, Confucian civic language, popular religion, Orthodoxy, and Japanese shrines. The Philippines remains Catholic without becoming a Vatican dependency.</p>"},
      {id:"integral",title:"The integral-territory exception",html:"<p>Chōsen, Formosa, and other integral territories fall under Japanese sovereignty. The non-assimilation principle therefore protects associated nations more clearly than cultures whose statehood Japan denies.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"The security limitation",html:"<p>Japan and national governments can restrict clergy, schools, funds, pilgrimage, publication, or foreign contacts when they allege subversion. The boundary between security and assimilation remains politically contested.</p>"},
      {id:"judgment",title:"Historical judgment",html:"<p>Supporters regard the doctrine as the religious counterpart of associated sovereignty. Critics regard it as pluralism granted from above and revocable when a protected institution becomes inconvenient.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"religion-geacps.html",kicker:"Wider order",label:"Religion in the Sphere"},{href:"japanese-reserve-supremacy.html",kicker:"Political authority",label:"Japanese Reserve Supremacy"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Constitutional limit",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"},{href:"state-shinto-outside-japan.html",kicker:"Principal test",label:"State Shinto outside Japan"},{href:"principal-associated-states-geacps.html",kicker:"National sovereignty",label:"Principal Associated States"}],
    facts:[["Doctrine","Religious non-assimilation"],["Scope","Associated and protected states"],["Japanese leadership","Political and strategic"],["Required conversion","None"],["Required duty","Loyalty, order, and treaty protection"],["Ceremonial attendance","Does not imply confession"],["Principal limitation","Security and integral-territory law"],["Formal title and promulgation","Open"]]
  }),

  "sacred-institutions-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Sacred Institutions in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Religious law",infoboxKicker:"Temples, churches, mosques, monasteries, schools, and religious property",
    lead:"Sacred institutions in the Co-Prosperity Sphere are the temples, shrines, churches, mosques, monasteries, seminaries, religious schools, charities, cemeteries, pilgrimage sites, courts, and endowed properties recognized under national or treaty law. Their protection allows member states to preserve distinct religious civilizations while their registration, property, education, and foreign contacts remain subject to public authority.",
    canon:"Protected Japanese shrines in foreign settlements, Malay Islamic institutions under Thai paramountcy, Mongolian Buddhist institutions, Vietnamese religious bodies, Catholic and Orthodox communities, and national religious jurisdiction are established. A common sacred-institutions convention is synthesis; registration, tax, property, inspection, dissolution, and appeal law remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"forms",title:"Institutional forms",html:"<p>A sacred institution may be a place of worship, school, court, charity, hospital, archive, pilgrimage estate, cemetery, monastic community, or legal corporation holding property for a religious body.</p>"},
      {id:"recognition",title:"Recognition",html:"<p>National law determines which bodies possess legal personality, may own land, teach children, solemnize marriages, receive donations, maintain courts, or invite foreign clergy.</p>"},
      {id:"catholic-governance",title:"Catholic ownership and governance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-f4q9n writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:23:21-06:00 --><p>A Catholic church, school, hospital, charity, cemetery, or seminary could be entrusted to a diocese, parish, religious institute, lay foundation, or other juridical body. Civil title might stand in the name of that body, a locally recognized corporation, trustees, or another approved holder. Registration and title protected the work in civil law. They did not erect a parish, create a religious institute, assign an ordinary, or determine which ecclesiastical authority governed the institution.</p><p>Institutional administration also differed from sacramental and canonical government. A hospital director, school head, bursar, board, or religious superior could exercise the powers granted by statutes, employment, and professional law. Public authorities could inspect safety, qualifications, accounts, labor practice, curricula outside reserved religious teaching, and the conditions attached to subsidies. Civil supervision did not confer authority over doctrine, sacraments, religious profession, or the internal government of an institute.</p><p>Property and governance records must therefore be read together: deeds, corporate registrations, canonical erection and entrustment instruments, constitutions, board minutes, visitations, accounts, subsidy terms, inspection reports, personnel files, dissolution orders, and appeals. Women religious, lay teachers, nurses, patients, pupils, donors, and local families can show who actually sustained a work and who received its services. Institutional survival may reflect pastoral attachment, competent administration, treaty privilege, state patronage, or accommodation in unequal proportions.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"treaty",title:"Treaty protection",html:"<p>Japanese shrines, Buddhist institutions, churches, schools, and cemeteries serving treaty residents can receive bilateral protection. Similar provisions may protect diaspora communities whose home states possess negotiating power.</p>"},
      {id:"property",title:"Property and endowment",html:"<p>Land, buildings, farms, rents, donations, company support, and foreign funds sustain institutions. Governments distinguish protected use from unrestricted political or commercial privilege.</p>"},
      {id:"schools",title:"Schools and charities",html:"<p>Religious institutions educate, heal, feed, shelter, publish, and preserve language as well as conduct worship. Their social services make regulation politically sensitive.</p>"},
      {id:"inspection",title:"Registration and inspection",html:"<p>Accounts, curricula, foreign funds, clergy lists, property, safety, and public-order compliance create ordinary points of state supervision. Exact procedure varies by national order.</p>"},
      {id:"conflict",title:"Conflict and restitution",html:"<p>War, border change, nationalization, company settlement, and population movement create disputes over abandoned or confiscated sacred property. No universal restitution rule is established.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Institutional autonomy",html:"<p>Protection is strongest when a religious body serves a recognized national community and avoids direct political confrontation. Autonomy narrows when rulers classify teaching or foreign contact as opposition.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"religious-nonassimilation-geacps.html",kicker:"Legal doctrine",label:"Religious Non-assimilation Doctrine"},{href:"religious-family-law-geacps.html",kicker:"Schools and personal status",label:"Religious Schools and Family Law"},{href:"foreign-religious-relations-geacps.html",kicker:"External ties",label:"Foreign Religious Relations"},{href:"buddhist-settlement-united-mongolia.html",kicker:"Buddhist example",label:"Buddhist Settlement in United Mongolia"},{href:"religion-in-vietnam.html",kicker:"Plural national example",label:"Religion in Vietnam"}],
    facts:[["Institutions","Worship · school · charity · court · cemetery · pilgrimage"],["Legal authority","National governments"],["Treaty protection","Available for recognized resident communities"],["Property","National law and bilateral guarantees"],["Common supervision","Registration · accounts · curricula · foreign funds"],["Uniform restitution law","None"],["Principal political test","Institutional autonomy versus security"],["Complete national statutes","Open"]]
  }),

  "religious-family-law-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Religious Schools and Family Law in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Personal law",infoboxKicker:"Education, marriage, inheritance, and household status",
    lead:"Religious schools and family law in the Co-Prosperity Sphere are governed through national settlements that recognize different combinations of civil law, customary authority, clergy, religious courts, schools, and household registration. Catholic, Muslim, Buddhist, Orthodox, Shinto, Confucian, and local institutions do not receive one uniform jurisdiction; their acts must be recognized by the state responsible for nationality and civil status.",
    canon:"Malay Islamic authority over family law, mosques, schools, and custom; Catholic and Buddhist national traditions; inherited nationality; mixed-nationality households; religious schools; and national civil authority are established. Common choice-of-law rules, marriage registers, divorce recognition, adoption, inheritance, and school inspection are synthesis or open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"schools",title:"Religious schools",html:"<p>Parish schools, temple schools, monastic education, Islamic schools, seminaries, and community institutions teach religion alongside language, literacy, history, and social discipline.</p>"},
      {id:"curriculum",title:"Curriculum and recognition",html:"<p>National authorities determine whether a religious school satisfies ordinary educational requirements and whether its certificates permit entry to public examinations or higher study.</p>"},
      {id:"marriage",title:"Marriage",html:"<p>Clergy or religious officials may solemnize marriages where national law recognizes their authority. Mixed-nationality and mixed-religion marriages require civil registration capable of traveling across borders.</p>"},
      {id:"catholic-status",title:"Catholic marriage and school status",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-r2k7d writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:20:35-06:00 --><p>Catholic marriage and civil household status answered different legal questions. Civil registration established the effects recognized by a state for nationality, inheritance, taxation, residence, legitimacy, and custody. Canonical records established baptismal status, freedom to marry, consent, form, permissions or dispensations, and the sacramental or canonical judgment made by competent Church authority. Civil recognition did not itself determine canonical validity, and an ecclesiastical judgment did not automatically alter a civil register.</p><p>A mixed-nationality or mixed-religion household could therefore require a marriage entry in a parish or other church register, one or more civil registrations, translations, proof of nationality and domicile, and later records concerning children. Disputes over baptism, religious education, custody, burial, or inheritance involved the rights and duties recognized by each legal order. Exact Catholic faculties, mixed-marriage discipline, national statutes, and cross-border recognition rules remain open.</p><p>A parish or religious school likewise held an ecclesiastical mission and a civil educational status that could overlap without merging. National authorities could inspect health, safety, teacher qualifications, ordinary curriculum, and certificate standards. They did not thereby acquire authority to write Catholic doctrine or compel pupils to profess it. School registers, inspection reports, fee and subsidy records, admissions rules, correspondence with parents, and accounts from pupils, lay teachers, and women religious are needed to show whether recognition protected education or made access dependent on coercion or privilege.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"personal",title:"Personal and customary law",html:"<p>Some Muslim and customary jurisdictions retain family, inheritance, guardianship, and community authority. Other states place the same questions principally in civil courts while recognizing religious rites.</p>"},
      {id:"children",title:"Children and education",html:"<p>Nationality law assigns one public nationality, commonly paternal, while parents may contest baptism, religious upbringing, school, language, custody, and burial across two traditions.</p>"},
      {id:"conversion",title:"Conversion and civil status",html:"<p>Conversion may change religious obligations without automatically changing nationality, residence, marriage validity, or inherited property. Exact effects depend upon national law.</p>"},
      {id:"recognition",title:"Cross-border recognition",html:"<p>A marriage, divorce, adoption, clerical judgment, or school certificate valid in one state may require civil confirmation in another. There is no common religious court of appeal.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Family law and sovereignty",html:"<p>Control of family and school law allows a state to claim that Japanese leadership has not dissolved its civilization. It can also preserve unequal sex, sectarian, or communal rules beneath the language of national protection.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"mixed-nationality-families-geacps.html",kicker:"Cross-national households",label:"Mixed-nationality Families"},{href:"teacher-training-textbook-policy-geacps.html",kicker:"School policy",label:"Teacher Training and Textbook Policy"},{href:"sacred-institutions-geacps.html",kicker:"Institutional context",label:"Sacred Institutions"},{href:"thailand.html",kicker:"Malay Islamic compact",label:"Kingdom of Thailand"},{href:"religion-geacps.html",kicker:"Wider order",label:"Religion in the Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Common Sphere family code","None"],["Religious schools","Recognized under national law"],["Civil effect of marriage","Requires state recognition"],["Personal law","Varies by nation and community"],["Child nationality","Governed by national law, commonly paternal"],["Common religious court","None"],["Cross-border acts","May require civil confirmation"],["Detailed choice-of-law rules","Open"]]
  }),

  "military-chaplaincy-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Military Chaplaincy in the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Military religion",infoboxKicker:"Religious care in Japanese and associated armed forces",
    lead:"Military chaplaincy in the Co-Prosperity Sphere is the national provision of clergy, ritual specialists, worship, burial, moral instruction, and pastoral care for armed forces serving within a Japanese-led defense system. Because the Sphere has no common religion, chaplaincy follows the service member's national force, treaty contingent, local community, and faith rather than one imperial ecclesiastical command.",
    canon:"Distinct national religions, Japanese units abroad, associated-state armed forces, national chaplaincy requirements, religious burial, and non-imposition of State Shinto are established or directly required by the plural defense order. Common chaplaincy regulations, corps, ranks, deployment ratios, insignia, and interfaith procedures remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"national",title:"National organization",html:"<p>Japanese, Philippine, Thai, Burmese, Mongolian, Russian, Chinese, and other forces organize religious care under their own service law. No Sphere chaplain-general commands all traditions.</p>"},
      {id:"japanese",title:"Japanese service rites",html:"<p>Japanese formations preserve imperial, Shinto, Buddhist, memorial, and secular service practices according to Japanese law. Their presence abroad does not convert the host population.</p>"},
      {id:"associated",title:"Associated contingents",html:"<p>Catholic priests, Buddhist monks or chaplains, Muslim religious personnel, Orthodox clergy, and other recognized ministers accompany or serve national contingents where doctrine and manpower permit.</p>"},
      {id:"catholic-competence",title:"Catholic chaplaincy competence",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-y7b2k writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:23:21-06:00 --><p>A Catholic military appointment and an ecclesiastical mission were separate acts. Service law could give a chaplain rank, pay, transport, access, quarters, and duties. The competent Catholic authority supplied canonical assignment and the faculties required for ministry. A defense ministry or combined command could not confer sacred order, extend a priest's sacramental competence, or make a Philippine service appointment a jurisdiction over Catholics in every allied force.</p><p>The applicable ordinary could be territorial, military, personal, or otherwise provided by Catholic law; the exact Sphere arrangements remain open. Religious superiors retained governance of members of their institutes within their competence. Combined operations consequently required records of rite, home authority, military assignment, faculties, unit, duration, and any delegation. Access granted by a host base did not transfer a local parish or diocese into the military chain of command.</p><p>Commanders could regulate movement, communications, quarantine, classified areas, and immediate safety. These powers did not turn confession or protected pastoral counsel into an intelligence channel, direct a sacrament, or authorize compulsory worship. Chaplains and medical staff needed procedures for casualties, death notification, remains, sacred objects, registers, and communication with families and home churches. Service regulations, faculty documents, chaplain logs, command orders, hospital and burial records, complaints, and testimony from ordinary personnel are needed to show how the formal settlement operated.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bases",title:"Bases and local communities",html:"<p>Permanent bases require chapels, shrines, prayer space, cemeteries, food practice, holidays, and liaison with local sacred institutions. Treaty law divides responsibility between force and host.</p>"},
      {id:"care",title:"Pastoral functions",html:"<p>Worship, confession or counsel, family liaison, hospital visitation, morale, ethical instruction, death notification, burial, and memorialization form the ordinary work.</p>"},
      {id:"mixed",title:"Mixed operations",html:"<p>Combined commands coordinate schedules, burial recovery, dietary needs, sacred objects, and access to clergy without requiring a common theology.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Clergy and security",html:"<p>Chaplains may possess confidential pastoral roles while remaining officers, contractors, or approved civilians inside a security institution. Exact privilege and reporting duties vary.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"War, occupation, and legitimacy",html:"<p>Respect for local religion can distinguish a treaty force from an occupying army in official presentation. Abuses, coerced rites, or contempt for burial can delegitimize the entire security guarantee.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"religion-geacps.html",kicker:"Religious context",label:"Religion in the Sphere"},{href:"five-power-strategic-defense-systems.html",kicker:"Military context",label:"Five-Power Strategic Defense Systems"},{href:"foreign-religious-relations-geacps.html",kicker:"Ecclesiastical ties",label:"Foreign Religious Relations"},{href:"christianity-japanese-micronesia.html",kicker:"Japanese-territory example",label:"Christianity in Japanese Micronesia"},{href:"orbital-catholic-chaplaincy.html",kicker:"Later comparative institution",label:"Orbital Catholic Chaplaincy"}],
    facts:[["System","National chaplaincies within common defense"],["Common chaplain-general","None"],["Japanese forces","Japanese religious and memorial practice"],["Associated forces","Clergy suited to national traditions"],["Base requirements","Worship · burial · diet · holiday · liaison"],["Primary functions","Pastoral care · morale · hospital · memorial"],["Confidentiality and reporting","Varies by service"],["Detailed corps and regulations","Open"]]
  }),

  "foreign-religious-relations-geacps": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"Foreign Religious Relations of the Co-Prosperity Sphere",category:"Religious diplomacy",infoboxKicker:"Cross-border hierarchies, pilgrimage, missions, and religious education",
    lead:"Foreign religious relations of the Co-Prosperity Sphere are the supervised ties connecting Catholics to Rome, Orthodox communities to foreign hierarchies, Buddhists to transnational monasteries and teachers, Muslims to pilgrimage and centers of scholarship, and Christian missions or diaspora bodies to institutions outside Japanese Asia. These ties are treated as religious rights, diplomatic channels, and possible avenues of foreign political influence at the same time.",
    canon:"Filipino Catholicism, Russian Orthodoxy, transnational Buddhist and Islamic communities, foreign religious ties as a necessary legal question, Japanese non-assimilation, and national control of diplomacy are established. Visa rules, concordats, pilgrimage quotas, mission law, financial reporting, censorship, and incidents remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"forms",title:"Forms of relation",html:"<p>Appointment, correspondence, pilgrimage, study, donations, missions, publishing, relics, conferences, charity, and recognition of clergy connect a national community to institutions abroad.</p>"},
      {id:"rome",title:"The Holy See and Catholic communities",html:"<p>The Philippines and smaller Catholic populations recognize a universal church whose center lies outside every political bloc. Governments negotiate how episcopal appointment, education, charity, and diplomacy coexist with national loyalty.</p>"},
      {id:"catholic-relations",title:"Catholic communion and diplomacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-x8m4h writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:20:35-06:00 --><p>Several acts commonly described as relations with Rome remained legally distinct. The pope or a competent ecclesiastical authority provided canonical appointment or mission. A government granted civil recognition, accepted diplomatic credentials, issued travel documents, or negotiated property and educational guarantees. A concordat, where one existed, was an agreement between public authorities; it did not confer sacred order or make episcopal jurisdiction a delegation from the state.</p><p>The Philippine hierarchy governed the churches committed to it and could provide personnel, education, finance, or fraternal assistance elsewhere in the Sphere. Such cooperation did not give it ordinary jurisdiction over every Catholic community in Japanese Asia. Local ordinaries, mission superiors, religious institutes, Eastern Catholic authorities, and the Holy See retained the competencies supplied by their own law. Host governments could regulate entry, landholding, schools, hospitals, publishing, donations, and foreign exchange while remaining unable to determine sacraments or ecclesial communion.</p><p>Foreign correspondence or receipt of Roman funds did not alone prove political direction. Evidence of clandestine influence required the responsible office, instructions, intermediaries, accounts, recipients, conditions, and the action taken. Appointment files, diplomatic notes, mission and institute archives, bank and customs records, censorship orders, school and hospital accounts, and testimony from clergy, women religious, lay associations, patients, and pupils could distinguish ordinary communion and charity from covert state activity. Durable Catholic institutions might reflect local attachment, negotiated protection, foreign privilege, or accommodation in different proportions.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"orthodox",title:"Orthodox jurisdictions",html:"<p>Russian communities in the northern Sphere and Manchukuo maintain Orthodox worship amid divided Russian states. Ecclesiastical allegiance can carry implications for monarchy, exile, and foreign claims.</p>"},
      {id:"buddhist",title:"Buddhist networks",html:"<p>Monasteries, teachers, texts, pilgrimage, and scholarship connect Japanese, Chinese, Mongolian, Tibetan, Thai, Burmese, Vietnamese, and other Buddhist traditions without creating one centralized Buddhist authority.</p>"},
      {id:"islam",title:"Islamic pilgrimage and scholarship",html:"<p>Uyghur, Malay, Indonesian, and other Muslim communities require access to learning, pilgrimage, charity, and wider religious correspondence. Travel and foreign exchange place these practices inside national and Japanese security systems.</p>"},
      {id:"missions",title:"Missions and education",html:"<p>Foreign missions may operate churches, schools, clinics, presses, and charities where host law permits. Their social institutions can outlast their original foreign sponsorship.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Security review",html:"<p>Governments monitor appointments, money, publications, travel, and contacts when they fear espionage, separatism, revolutionary organization, or rival-bloc patronage.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"Religious diplomacy",html:"<p>Clergy and sacred institutions can carry messages where formal diplomacy is strained. Japan's willingness to preserve such ties supports its guardianship claim while limiting complete strategic autonomy.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"catholic-church.html",kicker:"Universal Catholic authority",label:"Catholic Church"},{href:"orthodox-revival.html",kicker:"Orthodox context",label:"Orthodox Revival"},{href:"buddhist-settlement-united-mongolia.html",kicker:"Buddhist national settlement",label:"Buddhist Settlement in United Mongolia"},{href:"sacred-institutions-geacps.html",kicker:"Domestic institutions",label:"Sacred Institutions"},{href:"religious-nonassimilation-geacps.html",kicker:"Legal foundation",label:"Religious Non-assimilation Doctrine"}],
    facts:[["Relations","Appointment · pilgrimage · study · mission · charity · publication"],["Catholic external center","Holy See"],["Orthodox issue","Divided Russian jurisdictions"],["Buddhist form","Multiple transnational networks"],["Islamic ties","Pilgrimage, scholarship, and charity"],["Host authority","National governments under security limits"],["Political function","Diplomacy and potential foreign influence"],["Concordats and travel schedules","Open"]]
  }),

  "state-shinto-outside-japan": geacpsReligionArticle({
    title:"State Shinto outside Japan",category:"Shinto history",infoboxKicker:"Japanese shrines and imperial rites in treaty settlements",
    lead:"State Shinto outside Japan consists of the shrines, imperial observances, military rites, schools, associations, and community institutions maintained by Japanese residents, forces, and corporations in associated states. These institutions receive treaty or corporate protection and publicly express Japanese identity and loyalty; they do not constitute the state religion of Manchukuo, Thailand, the Philippines, or other foreign members of the Co-Prosperity Sphere.",
    canon:"State Shinto as a Japanese institution, its strong presence in Manchukuoan Japanese settlements, military communities, and corporate cities, treaty protection, official courtesy, and non-imposition upon associated peoples are established. Shrine lists, governing bureau, clergy, finances, attendance rules, postwar reforms, and disputes remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"status",title:"Legal status",html:"<p>Outside integral Japan, a shrine is a protected institution of Japanese residents rather than an organ of the host state's national religion. Its exact legal personality rests on treaty, property, concession, or corporate law.</p>"},
      {id:"settlers",title:"Settler communities",html:"<p>Long-established Japanese districts maintain rites marking imperial festivals, ancestors, war dead, migration, school life, corporate service, and local community identity.</p>"},
      {id:"military",title:"Military and base practice",html:"<p>Japanese forces abroad preserve service rites and memorials within bases and cemeteries. Host populations are not required to adopt them under the mature non-assimilation settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"corporate",title:"Corporate cities",html:"<p>Mantetsu and other Japanese employers may support shrines alongside schools, clinics, housing, clubs, and cemeteries. Religious and corporate welfare institutions become parts of one settler environment.</p>"},
      {id:"courtesy",title:"Official attendance",html:"<p>Host officials may attend important observances as diplomatic courtesy, just as Japanese officials attend royal, Buddhist, Muslim, Catholic, Orthodox, or national rites. Courtesy does not establish confession.</p>"},
      {id:"participation",title:"Participation and religious profession",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-c-20260816-d3m8v writer=c kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:23:21-06:00 --><p>The civil description of an observance did not settle its religious meaning for Catholic participants. A memorial, oath, bow, offering, invocation, school act, or shrine visit had to be judged by its words, gestures, object, required intention, and surrounding instruction. Catholic authorities could distinguish diplomatic or civic courtesy from an act of worship within their competence. A host government, employer, or military office could not make that ecclesiastical judgment simply by classifying every rite as patriotic.</p><p>Formal voluntariness also required practical freedom to abstain. Corporate housing, promotion, school grades, military evaluation, access to rations or welfare, and community standing could impose serious pressure without a written conversion rule. Japanese residents retained the right to maintain their institutions, while Catholic and other non-Shinto residents retained their own religious obligations. Treaty protection for a shrine did not authorize punishment of a person who declined a religious act.</p><p>The evidentiary record includes service and school manuals, ceremony scripts, attendance lists, contribution schedules, employment rules, exemption requests, alternative observances, disciplinary files, host-state correspondence, and instructions issued by bishops, chaplains, or other religious authorities. Accounts from pupils, workers, spouses, local officials, and minority congregations can identify pressures omitted from shrine or corporate records. Exact attendance rules, exemptions, disputes, and postwar reforms remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits and resentment",html:"<p>Land privilege, school ritual, military presence, settler influence, and memories of wartime coercion can make a formally voluntary shrine politically contentious.</p>"},
      {id:"integral",title:"Contrast with integral territories",html:"<p>In Chōsen, Formosa, Karafuto, and other integral regions, State Shinto operates under Japanese sovereignty rather than foreign treaty protection. The legal distinction does not guarantee equal cultural freedom.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Position by 1985",html:"<p>State Shinto remains visible wherever Japanese communities are permanent. Associated-state legitimacy increasingly depends upon keeping those institutions protected but clearly separate from national compulsory religion.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"religious-nonassimilation-geacps.html",kicker:"Governing limit",label:"Religious Non-assimilation Doctrine"},{href:"manchukuo.html",kicker:"Principal foreign example",label:"Manchukuo"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Domestic contrast",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"},{href:"japan.html",kicker:"Sovereign religion",label:"Empire of Japan"},{href:"sacred-institutions-geacps.html",kicker:"Treaty protection",label:"Sacred Institutions"}],
    facts:[["Status outside Japan","Protected Japanese institution"],["Principal communities","Settlers · forces · corporations"],["Host-state religion","No"],["Compulsory host attendance","None under mature doctrine"],["Common functions","Imperial observance · memorial · community rite"],["Legal basis","Treaty, concession, property, or corporate law"],["Integral-territory contrast","Direct Japanese sovereignty"],["Shrine registry and administration","Open"]]
  })
});

const addGeacpsReligionRelated = (slug,item) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article) return;
  article.related = article.related || [];
  if (!article.related.some(existing => existing.href === item.href)) article.related.push(item);
};

