const addGeacpsReligionSection = (slug,beforeId,section) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article || article.sections.some(existing => existing.id === section.id)) return;
  const index = article.sections.findIndex(existing => existing.id === beforeId);
  if (index < 0) article.sections.push(section);
  else article.sections.splice(index,0,section);
};

addGeacpsReligionSection("religion-geacps","catholic-communion",{id:"jurisdictional-map",title:"Jurisdictional map",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-4vni0 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:54:11-06:00 --><p>The absence of a common cult did not remove religion from government. It distributed responsibility among legal orders. Japan could protect its nationals, negotiate access, maintain its forces, and invoke the political or strategic agreements of the Sphere. It did not thereby acquire ordinary power to recognize clergy, register a local charity, decide a marriage, license a school, or transfer sacred property inside an associated state.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Ordinary authority</th><th>Japanese connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Japan and integral territories</td><td>Japanese public law, courts, ministries, territorial and local government, and recognized religious institutions</td><td>State Shinto formed part of the Japanese civic-sacral order; other traditions operated under Japanese sovereignty</td></tr><tr><td>Associated or protected state</td><td>Its sovereign, ministers, legislature, courts, police, local authorities, and religious bodies</td><td>Tokyo used treaty, diplomacy, guarantees, finance, and strategic bargaining rather than a universal religious office</td></tr><tr><td>Japanese settlement or company district abroad</td><td>Host-state law governing land, permits, labor, public order, schools, and legal personality</td><td>Japanese treaty protection, employers, associations, schools, shrines, and consular or diplomatic representation supported resident institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Japanese base or formation abroad</td><td>Applicable treaty and host jurisdiction outside the reserved installation; Japanese command within its assigned competence</td><td>Service rites and chaplaincy remained institutions of the Japanese force rather than the host population</td></tr><tr><td>Transnational communion or network</td><td>Religious authority over doctrine and internal government, subject to each state's civil settlement</td><td>Japan could supervise political and security effects without becoming the source of sacramental authority</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A dispute therefore required identification of both the act and the competent institution. Property seizure, school accreditation, foreign remittance, pilgrimage travel, clerical appointment, cemetery access, military discipline, and compulsory attendance did not belong to one Sphere religious code. Japanese intervention was strongest where a dispute affected treaty residents, bases, transport corridors, foreign alignment, or public order. That reserve influence was substantial, but it normally operated through the national government whose law had to implement the result.</p><p>Post-preservationist policy favored this allocation because working national and local institutions could preserve worship, relief, education, family records, buildings, and public peace without reconstructing every earlier jurisdiction or linguistic community. Cultural recognition did not automatically create political autonomy. The functional test nevertheless favored institutions already reinforced by Japanese credit, security, transport, and diplomatic access. A community whose land had been taken, school subordinated, or clergy screened could appear administratively weak because the imperial settlement had first deprived it of the means by which autonomy would have worked.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
addGeacpsReligionSection("state-shinto-outside-japan","limits",{id:"jurisdiction-maintenance",title:"Jurisdiction and maintenance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-d70l9 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:54:11-06:00 --><p>A Japanese shrine abroad combined several relationships that were easily mistaken for one jurisdiction. The host state supplied the law of landholding, legal personality, construction, fire and health safety, policing, taxation, labor, schooling, and public assembly. Japanese diplomacy protected the resident community and pressed treaty claims. A company, military command, residents' association, school body, or shrine custodian supplied property, staff, maintenance, ceremony, and records according to the powers each actually possessed.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Matter</th><th>Implementing institution</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Site and building</td><td>Owner or concession holder, host land and building authorities, local utilities, and shrine custodian</td><td>Treaty protection did not make the parcel Japanese sovereign territory</td></tr><tr><td>Finance and upkeep</td><td>Resident contributions, employer support, associations, endowments, or other lawfully approved sources</td><td>Corporate subsidy could carry employment pressure without creating a host-state church</td></tr><tr><td>School and civic observance</td><td>School managers, teachers, community bodies, and the host authorities competent over education and public order</td><td>Japanese identity did not erase the distinction between instruction, courtesy, and compelled religious participation</td></tr><tr><td>Military rite</td><td>Japanese command and base institutions within the applicable agreement</td><td>A service order bound the formation only within its lawful command competence</td></tr><tr><td>Public ceremony and official attendance</td><td>Shrine or community organizer with host permits, security, and diplomatic liaison</td><td>Courtesy by a host official neither transferred sovereignty nor proved personal confession</td></tr><tr><td>Complaint and remedy</td><td>Employer process, school appeal, host administration or court, and diplomatic negotiation as the issue required</td><td>Japanese protection could not be treated as automatic immunity from host law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The legal distinction was most difficult in a corporate town or permanent settler district. The same enterprise might control housing, wages, school places, transport, clinics, and the land around a shrine. Refusal to contribute or attend could therefore carry social or material costs without a compulsory-religion statute. Host regulation remained real, but a government dependent upon Japanese credit, rolling stock, employment, or diplomatic support did not bargain on equal terms.</p><p>Post-preservationist officials treated a maintained shrine, archive, cemetery, school collection, or voluntary festival as legitimate diaspora heritage. They resisted converting long residence and inherited memory into a claim to Japanese sovereignty over Manchukuoan or other associated-state soil. That position preserved a workable legal boundary, yet it could understate how settler land privilege, wartime seizure, company government, and Japanese strategic protection had made the community permanent. Cultural survival and political title were distinct; neither made the history of possession neutral.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
